 "- **Normalization** — Canonicalizing text: unicode forms, case, whitespace, encodings. The same word in two encodings is two words to a naive pipeline.",
 "- **Regular expressions** — Pattern matching for extraction and cleaning: emails, dates, HTML tags, whitespace runs. The precision instrument — and a readability hazard when overgrown.",
 "- **Sentence segmentation** — Splitting text into sentences. Harder than it looks: abbreviations, decimals, ellipses. Errors here propagate to every downstream step.",
 "- **Tokenization (classical)** — Splitting text into words/subwords. The classical word level is largely superseded by subword methods — but the concept organizes everything.",
 "- **Stopwords** — High-frequency words (the, is, at) often removed for classical models. Helps bag-of-words focus; harms anything where function words carry signal (authorship, some semantics).",
 "- **Stemming vs lemmatization** — Crude suffix-chopping vs dictionary-based base forms. Both normalize vocabulary; both can merge distinct meanings — know which you applied.",
 "- **What subword models made obsolete** — Heavy normalization, aggressive stopword removal, manual vocabularies. The tokenizer handles much of it — but not encoding errors or boilerplate.",
 "- **Boilerplate removal** — Stripping headers, footers, navigation, and template text from scraped documents. The difference between a clean corpus and a menu-text corpus.",
 "- **Encoding hell** — Mojibake from misdecoded bytes. Detect early (ftfy-style fixes exist); a corpus with encoding errors teaches the model your corruption.",
